PT J
AU Esposito, A
   Chiariello, AM
AF Esposito, A.
   Chiariello, A. M.
TI Polymer physics of genome folding: A predictive framework
SO NUOVO CIMENTO C-COLLOQUIA AND COMMUNICATIONS IN PHYSICS
LA English
DT Article; Proceedings Paper
CT 111th National Congress of the Italian-Physical-Society (SIF)
CY SEP 22-26, 2025
CL Palermo, ITALY
SP Italian Phys Soc
ID DOMAINS
AB The three-dimensional organization of the genome plays a key role in controlling cellular activity by shaping contacts between genes and their regulatory elements. Here we present a framework that combines Polymer Physics and Machine Learning to reconstruct and predict chromosome folding from experimental contact data. As an application, we study the Sox9 genomic region in mouse and show that the inferred polymer model accurately reproduces the observed contact patterns. We then introduce genomic mutations in silico within the inferred model and derive the resulting chromosome folding directly from polymer physics. The predicted changes in contact patterns are consistent with independent experimental observations and illustrate how physics-based modeling can provide predictive insight into the structural impact of genomic mutations.
