TI Corona Effect Detection in Energized Polymeric Insulators Using Machine Learning and Ultrasonic Emissions
SO IEEE LATIN AMERICA TRANSACTIONS
LA Portuguese
DT Article
DE artificial neural network; machine learning; multilayer perceptron; support vector machines; polymeric insulator; ultrasound; corona; monitoring; diagnostics
ID NETWORKS
AB The occurrence of corona effect is an important indication that an insulator probably has malfunction. Thus, the corona detection can help to diagnose problems in insulators, which might cause serious damage to the power distribution system, such as the sudden interruption of service. Machine learning techniques are used to classifying patterns into categories. This paper evaluated the application of MLP (multilayer perceptron) and SVM (support vector machines) for corona detection in high voltage polymeric insulators using ultrasonic emissions. To perform the capture of these data, a specific electronic circuit was developed in order to capture the ultrasonic signals tuned in the 40 kHz. The results indicate that both methods offer high generalizability and looks to be promising techniques for this type of problem, using ultrasonic frequencies as the data source.
