TI Research on CD14 macromolecule and esophageal squamous cell carcinoma biomarkers based on electrochemical sensors: decoding of ferritin immune crosstalk hub gene
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma; Ferroptosis; Immune microenvironment; Machine learning; Hub genes
AB Among digestive tract malignancies, esophageal cancer stands out for its aggressiveness, and ESCC represents the dominant histopathological form in populations across East Asia. The clinical necessity of continuous dynamic monitoring of metabolic biomarkers, and electrochemical sensors can provide technical support for these needs. The present investigation furnished a systematic dissection of the ferroptosis-immunity regulatory axis in ESCC by integrating transcriptomic profiling with multi-algorithm machine learning. Notwithstanding progress in operative procedures, cytotoxic chemotherapy, and radiotherapeutic protocols, the proportion of ESCC patients surviving beyond five years persists at less than 20%, largely because of delayed diagnosis, aggressive locoregional spread, and a high propensity for lymph node dissemination. Elucidating the molecular underpinnings of ESCC tumorigenesis therefore holds considerable promise for enhancing early detection and enabling the design of innovative treatment paradigms.
