TI Anodic interfacial challenges and engineering strategies in solid-state lithium batteries with composite polymer solid electrolytes
SO NANO ENERGY
LA English
DT Review
DE Composite polymer solid electrolyte; Li anode; Interface stability; Li dendrite
ID ION-CONDUCTING MEMBRANE; IN-SALT ELECTROLYTE; HYBRID ELECTROLYTE; DENDRITIC GROWTH; METAL BATTERIES; INORGANIC NANOCOMPOSITES; MOLECULAR-DYNAMICS; CURRENT COLLECTOR; LI DEPOSITION; STABILITY
AB Composite polymer solid electrolytes (CPSEs) have demonstrated significant potential in solid-state lithium batteries (SSLBs), attributed to their superior mechanical properties and high ionic conductivity. Nevertheless, the interfacial issues between CPSEs and Li anodes, encompassing contact issues, ionic transport dynamics, detrimental side reactions, and Li dendrite formation, remain critical challenges that significantly restrict electrochemical performance. This review comprehensively summarizes the recent advances in electrolyte engineering, interface engineering, and Li anode engineering, focusing on the critical roles of chemical composition, structural regulation, and fabrication techniques in addressing interfacial challenges. Combined experimental and theoretical analyses unveil the underlying mechanisms of these interfacial issues. Finally, future research directions are proposed, underscoring the necessity of interdisciplinary research paradigms that combine machine learning algorithms with advanced characterization techniques and multiscale modeling to overcome existing bottlenecks and establish a robust framework for the rational design of high-performance SSLBs.
