TI Deep reinforcement learning-based energy management strategy for unmanned aerial vehicles powered by solar cells and lithium polymer battery
SO JOURNAL OF ENERGY STORAGE
LA English
DT Article
DE Solar powered UAVs; Hybrid energy system; Maximum power point tracking; Deep reinforcement learning; Energy management strategy
ID DESIGN
AB The enhancement of solar energy utilization efficiency is pivotal for reducing carbon emissions and augmenting operational performance of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV) powered by solar cells and lithium polymer battery. This study presents an energy management strategy based on the deep reinforcement learning method (DRLEMS) to enhance the efficiency of solar energy harvesting and utilization. The core is to handle the maximum power point tracking of solar cells and adaptive power allocation between solar cells and lithium polymer battery. The dedicated hybrid energy system topology is architected to harmonize the operation of solar cells and lithium polymer battery, and it enables efficient energy harvesting from solar irradiance and provides continuous power supply. DRLEMS adopts the deep deterministic policy gradient algorithm to control the output current and voltage of the DC-DC converter, and further adjust the operating point. The simulation and experimental results demonstrate that DRLEMS can significantly improve the utilization efficiency of solar energy for solar-powered UAVs. Compared with the state machine strategy integrated with perturbation and observation method, DRLEMS can improve the solar energy utilization efficiency by 8.3% and 9.1%, respectively. This research presents a viable pathway to advance the energy efficiency and prolonged endurance of solar-powered UAVs, thereby supporting the broader adoption and development of renewable clean energy in aviation.
