TI Latent chemospace cartography of polymer emitters via machine learning and fragment-recombinative design heuristics
SO SYNTHETIC METALS
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Polymers; Organic solar cells; Structure-property relationship
ID ORGANIC SOLAR-CELLS; DONOR
AB An automated process is used to systematically build a diverse library of novel polymers, and machine learning algorithms are then used to predict the light emission behavior of these polymers. The polymer library is effectively visualized using t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE). After that, the polymers are screened according to their expected values, paying particular attention to those that show red-shifted emission characteristics. The polymers' property landscape is examined using the Structure Activity Landscape Index (SALI), which offers insightful information about the links between their structure and properties. To find trends and connections between the polymers, clustering and chemical similarity investigations are also carried out. Synthetic Accessibility (SA) score, which indicates the probability of successful synthesis, is calculated for each chosen polymer in order to evaluate the practical viability of synthesis. The majority of polymers have low synthetic accessibility scores, indicating ease of synthesis.
