TI Analyzing the immune related central genes of DPP8 macromolecules and potential diagnostic biomarkers through electrochemical sensors: Identifying the immune microenvironment and central genes in osteoporosis
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Osteoporosis; Immune microenvironment; Hub genes; Machine learning; Biomarkers
AB Osteoporosis represents a metabolic bone disease marked by compromised bone density and structural integrity. Emerging evidence indicates that immunological imbalance contributes to its development; however, the immunological landscape within osteoporotic skeletal tissue remains largely unexplored. We sought to comprehensively profile immune microenvironment alterations and discover pivotal immune-associated genes that could serve as diagnostic indicators for osteoporosis. Thirty-seven genes exhibited significant expression differences between patient and healthy cohorts. Immune profiling uncovered notable shifts in five cell populations: MDSC, CD56bright NK cells, Tfh cells, and effector memory CD4 T cells showed reduced abundance, while eosinophil levels were elevated in diseased samples. Machine learning integration yielded 16 candidate hub genes, among which DPP8, Y16709, and ZEB2 exhibited superior predictive performance (AUC exceeding 0.78). An integrated multi-gene classifier attained an AUC value of 0.874. Our findings delineate immune dysregulation patterns in osteoporosis and propose 16 genes with biomarker potential, offering mechanistic insights into immune-mediated bone pathology.
