   Broadbelt, Linda J.
TI Predicting ceiling temperature for recyclable polymer design using machine learning
SO CELL REPORTS SUSTAINABILITY
LA English
DT Article
ID SUBSTITUENT; LACTONE
AB Inadequate recycling rates create a critical demand for new recyclable materials. Intrinsically recyclable polymers (IRPs) can be chemically recycled under mild conditions through selective depolymerization to monomers, making them attractive alternatives to conventional plastics. Designing IRPs computationally requires knowledge of the thermodynamic ceiling temperature (TC), which is difficult to predict using density functional theory due to the interplay among structure, chain length, phase, and chemical interactions. Here, we report machine-learning models to predict TC and its components, enthalpy and entropy of polymerization, using the largest experimental dataset yet aggregated for these properties, covering ring-opening, vinyl-addition, and ionic polymerization types across various phases. Our feature set includes literature-informed descriptors and a pre-estimation of enthalpy of polymerization. For a specified monomer structure and phase/solvent combination, our models predict polymerization enthalpy, entropy, and TC with errors of 1.27 kcal/mol, 3.24 cal/mol/K, and 136 degrees C, respectively, facilitating IRP design for plastics recyclability.
