TI Polymer electrolytes for rechargeable Zn-air batteries: Emerging fabrication strategies and smart functional & machine learning perspectives
SO CURRENT OPINION IN ELECTROCHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Review
AB Zinc-air batteries (ZABs) are attractive energy storage systems due to their high energy density, low cost, and environmental compatibility. However, their practical use is limited by issues such as electrolyte leakage, dendrite growth, and interfacial instability. Polymer electrolytes (PEs) offer a promising solution, combining ionic conductivity with mechanical flexibility and enhanced safety. Recent advances include hydrogel electrolytes with excellent water retention, quasi-solid-state polymer electrolytes (QSSEs) with improved dimensional stability, and composite or hybrid systems incorporating nanomaterials or bio-based components to contribute to conductivity and durability. Electrospun nanofiber membranes further enhance ion transport through their high porosity and tunable microstructures. Emerging strategies, such as machine learning, accelerate electrolyte design by predicting crucial structure-property relationships, while shape memory polymers provide adaptive, self-healing functions for flexible and wearable ZABs. Thus, these developments demonstrate polymer electrolytes in enabling safe, efficient, and multifunctional ZABs, advancing their potential for next-generation sustainable energy storage applications.
