TI Design of polymers with higher atomization energy with help of machine learning and chemical space generation
SO MATERIALS TODAY COMMUNICATIONS
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Polymers; Atomization energy; Synthetic accessibility
ID ORGANIC SOLAR-CELLS; SMALL-MOLECULE ACCEPTORS; POLYVINYL-CHLORIDE; COMPLEXES; DONOR
AB Designing materials with desired properties is a challenging task, often hindered by the laborious and expensive nature of conventional experimental methods, especially for large-scale screening. To overcome these limitations, a more viable solution lies in the realm of machine learning (ML) guided exploration. In our current investigation, various ML models were employed to predict the atomization energy of polymers, Hist Gradient Boosting model has emerged as the most effective one. A comprehensive polymer database was generated, and ML model was used for rapid prediction of atomization energy. The generated database of polymers is visualized and analyzed. Structural behavior of chosen polymers is studied using chemical similarity analysis. Synthetic accessibility assessment that done using synthetic accessibility (SA) score has indicated that majority of selected polymers are easy to synthesize. This innovative approach proves to be highly beneficial for the virtual screening of materials, offering a more efficient and cost-effective means for designing and identifying polymers.
