TI Elastomer strategies for perovskite solar cells: A review from functional layer perspective
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Review
DE Elastomers; Perovskite solar cells; Mechanical stability; Defect passivation; Interface engineering
ID STABILITY; EFFICIENT; PERFORMANCE; ELECTRODE
AB Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have achieved power conversion efficiencies exceeding 27%, yet the inherent brittleness of perovskite crystals, interfacial delamination under mechanical stress, and environmental instability remain critical barriers to their commercial deployment, particularly in flexible and wearable applications. Elastomers, characterized by low elastic modulus, high extensibility, and dynamic bonding capabilities, have emerged as additives and structural components for addressing the aforementioned challenges. This review analyzes elastomer integration strategies in PSCs by functional layer location. The grain boundary incorporation for defect passivation and mechanical reinforcement, interfacial layers for strain dissipation and charge extraction, encapsulation for environmental protection and lead leakage suppression, and functionalized substrates for extreme mechanical deformation ability. In addition, by correlating molecular design principles, including dynamic covalent bonds, supramolecular interactions, and cross-linking architectures, with devicelevel performance metrics, we establish structure-property-performance relationships that guide rational elastomer design for specific functional requirements. Finally, perspectives on machine learning-accelerated material discovery and multifunctional elastomer integration toward commercially viable, mechanically resilient perovskite photovoltaics are discussed.
