TI DNA polymerase ε and δ variants drive mutagenesis in polypurine tracts in human tumors
SO CELL REPORTS
LA English
DT Article
ID MUTATIONAL PROCESSES; GERMLINE MUTATIONS; COLORECTAL-CANCER; MUTATOR PHENOTYPE; DOMAIN MUTATIONS; STRUCTURAL BASIS; REPLICATION; POLE; ORIGINS; IDENTIFICATION
AB Alterations in the exonuclease domain of DNA polymerase epsilon cause ultramutated cancers. These cancers accumulate AGA>ATA transversions; however, their genomic features beyond the trinucleotide motifs are obscure. We analyze the extended DNA context of ultramutation using whole-exome sequencing data from 524 endometrial and 395 colorectal tumors. We find that G>T transversions in POLE-mutant tumors predominantly affect sequences containing at least six consecutive purines, with a striking preference for certain positions within polypurine tracts. Using this signature, we develop a machine-learning classifier to identify tumors with hitherto unknown POLE drivers and validate two drivers, POLE-E978G and POLE-S461L, by functional assays in yeast. Unlike other pathogenic variants, the E978G substitution affects the polymerase domain of Pol epsilon. We further show that tumors with POLD1 drivers share the extended signature of POLE ultramutation. These findings expand the understanding of ultramutation mechanisms and highlight peculiar mutagenic properties of polypurine tracts in the human genome.
