TI Decoding polymer nano-heterogeneity: Deep-learning-driven extraction of nanostructural parameters from SAXS data
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
ID X-RAY-SCATTERING; SMALL-ANGLE SCATTERING
AB Fourth-generation synchrotron Small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) beamlines produce data at rates that render conventional manual analysis impractical. Here we present IDFNet, a deep-learning framework that directly predicts from raw SAXS curves two sorted characteristic lengths, Dmin and Dmax, which correspond to the smaller and larger of the crystalline and amorphous thicknesses while respecting Babinet's principle. On the test set, the predictions achieve mean absolute error below 0.5 nm and R-2 above 0.95. Using SHAP-based gradient explainability, we verify that the model relies on the low-q structure-factor region (q approximate to 0.05 angstrom(-1)), consistent with the structure factor region of SAXS curve, and not on artifacts. By learning physically meaningful features, IDFNet enables fully automated, real-time SAXS analysis (1.0 ms per curve), paving the way for smart beamline architectures with instantaneous experimental feedback.
