TI Harnessing porosity: A review on polymer-based porous structures for high-performance passive radiative cooling
SO MATERIALS TODAY ENERGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Passive daytime radiative cooling; Porous polymeric material; Machine learning; Material optimization; Pore-formation
ID INDUCED PHASE-SEPARATION; FILMS; TEMPERATURE; NONSOLVENT
AB Porous polymeric materials for passive daytime radiative cooling (PDRC) enable significant sub-ambient cooling without energy input by leveraging a synergistic mechanism: high solar reflectance to minimize solar heat absorption, high infrared emissivity to dissipate heat through atmospheric windows. This review provides theoretical foundations and technical guidance for the design and engineering of high-performance, sustainable porous polymeric PDRC materials. It elaborates on the photothermal regulation mechanism, clarifying the critical interplay between Mie scattering-which governs solar reflectance-and molecular vibrations that drive infrared emission. It summarizes various preparation strategies, such as phase separation and templating methods, along with their correlations to structure and performance. It introduces machine learning applications in reverse material design, lifespan prediction, and anti-soiling optimization to accelerate the development of materials. Practical implementations in areas including building cooling, personal thermal management, and wound dressings are reviewed to demonstrate real-world efficacy. Future breakthroughs, which would require a shift from optimizing individual materials toward establishing a multidimensional, synergistic innovation system encompassing intelligent response mechanisms, energy synergy, and sustainable material development, are also discussed.
