TI Nanoarchitectonics approach to heteroatom-doped activated carbon through electrophilic polymerization of phenothiazine for supercapacitor and rechargeable zinc-quinone cell applications
SO JOURNAL OF POWER SOURCES
LA English
DT Article
ID ENERGY; CAPACITANCE
AB Utilization of porous carbons in supercapacitors can be traced to several intrinsic properties including high surface area, intriguing pore systems, excellent electronic conductivity, and physicochemical stability. Despite the commonly acclaimed contribution of high surface area toward specific capacitance, we demonstrate herein that this long-held perception overlooks the significance of surface structuring through heteroatom doping. We report a designer approach to construct heteroatom-doped microporous carbon utilizing electrophilic polymerization of phenothiazine followed by a one-pot graphitization/activation step. The reported carbon demonstrated a high specific surface area of 2543 m(2) g(-1) and a specific capacitance of 545 F g(-1), with notable stability after 6000 charge-discharge cycles, retaining 89% of its original capacitance. Additionally, the high surface area carbon was utilized as a porous conductive host in a hydroquinone-zinc battery, demonstrating a specific capacity of similar to 450 mAh g(-1), approaching the theoretical capacity of 486.8 mAh g(-1) for hydroquinone. A Random Forest regressor machine learning model further outlined strong correlations between specific capacitance and micropore surface area, mesoporosity, and heteroatom doping.
