TI Data-driven prediction of crystal growth kinetics in polymer-stabilized amorphous drugs
SO JOURNAL OF APPLIED CRYSTALLOGRAPHY
LA English
DT Article
DE crystallization kinetics; amorphous pharmaceutical solids; physical stability; machine learning; data-driven modeling
ID CRYSTALLIZATION; NUCLEATION; INHIBITION; POLOXAMER; GLASSES; IMPACT
AB Amorphous solids have been developed as a good vehicle for delivering poorly water-soluble drugs in past decades. However, due to their thermodynamically unstable properties, amorphous solids tend to crystallize, thus negating their advantages in solubility and bioavailability. The purpose of this study was to develop a novel machine learning (ML)-integrated framework for predicting the crystallization kinetics of amorphous drugs doped with low-concentration polymer. Among these tested ML algorithms, our extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) model exhibited the highest predictive accuracy within the experimental dataset, achieving an R-2 of 0.76 and a root mean square error of 0.61. Shapley additive explanations revealed that the XGBoost model also exhibited satisfactory interpretability. Several critical features influencing crystallization of amorphous solid dispersions were identified, encompassing active pharmaceutical ingredient related molecular descriptors and polymer-specific properties. These findings were relevant for understanding the mechanisms of physical stability. More importantly, this AI-driven model has the potential advantage of reducing the workload of time-consuming long-term stability tests.
