TI Machine learning-optimized coarse-grained simulations reveal multicomponent solvent-regulated phase separation for precise control of porous polymers
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Porous materials; Phase separation; Molecular dynamics simulations; Machine learning-assisted force fields
ID NANOPARTICLES; MEMBRANES; DYNAMICS; DELIVERY
AB Precise control of pore structures in porous polymers is critical for optimizing their functional performance. Although third solvent-assisted phase separation has been experimentally demonstrated to promote pore interconnectivity and structural tunability, the molecular mechanisms governing this process remain largely unresolved. Here, we develop a machine learning-assisted dissipative particle dynamics (DPD) force fields that bridges all-atom-level accuracy and coarse-grained efficiency through data-driven force field generation. By training on structural descriptors derived from all-atom simulations, the optimized DPD force fields reliably reproduce intermolecular interactions in multicomponent solvent systems. Applying this optimized force field to polylactic acid ternary solvent systems, we reveal how the third solvent modulates phase separation behavior and propose a quantitative solvent-selection principle based on polarity and interaction energy. Experimental validation confirms the predictive capability of the model in guiding pore morphology control. Together, these results provide molecular-level insights and a practical framework for multicomponent solvent engineering, offering a pathway toward the rational fabrication advanced porous polymer materials with precisely controllable structures.
