TI Machine learning-guided discovery of polymer membranes for CO2 separation with genetic algorithm
SO JOURNAL OF MEMBRANE SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Membranes; Polymers; Gas separation; Inverse design; Genetic algorithm
ID FRACTIONAL FREE-VOLUME; GAS SEPARATION; TRANSPORT; FINGERPRINTS; PROPERTY
AB Designing polymer membranes with high gas permeability and selectivity is a difficult multi-task constrained problem due to the trade-off between these two properties. In this work, we present a machine learning (ML) driven genetic algorithm to tackle the design problem of polymer membranes for CO2 2 separation from N2 2 and O2. 2 . Using literature data of permeability for three gases, we constructed multiple ML models with different fingerprinting featurization schemes to predict gas permeabilities. Then, we employed a genetic algorithm to design new polymers and evaluated their performance using our ML models. We were able to identify new polymer membranes that are promising for both CO2/N2 2 /N 2 and CO2/O2 2 /O 2 separations. The top discovered polymers are predicted to have high glass transition temperatures. Similarly, the pyridine functionality was found in approximate to 20% of the predicted polymers. This framework can be used to design polymers for any application involving constrained optimization. Finally, we outlined the challenges and opportunities with using ML guided data-driven inverse design of polymers.
