TI Theory-guided machine learning for strength prediction of polymers at different temperatures and strain rates with physical information
SO MATERIALS TODAY COMMUNICATIONS
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Temperature and Strain rate; Theory-guided machine learning
ID DEPENDENT MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; NANOCOMPOSITES
AB Polymer materials, as the significant matrix for advanced composite materials, have unique properties, and are employed extensively in the aerospace industry. Meanwhile, the strength properties of polymers at different temperatures and strain rates concerns their safety use in a wide temperature range and dynamic load. In this study, a novel theory-guided machine learning (TGML) framework is developed to overcome the critical challenge of data scarcity inherent in conventional data-driven approaches. This is achieved by employing the validated physically-based theoretical models, derived from the proposed Force-Heat Equivalent Energy Density (FHEED) principle with physical information, to generate high-fidelity synthetic training data. The rigorous quantitative evaluation (including MSE, RMSE, MAE and R2) confirms that the TGML framework achieves high prediction accuracy on the tensile strength of polymers by comparison with experimental measurements. This data generation strategy constrained by physical information significantly enhances the model robustness and generalization ability compared to purely data-driven models. This work offers a reliable TGML method for strength prediction of polymers at different temperatures and strain rates, and can be extended to the accurate prediction of mechanical properties of polymer composites in extreme conditions.
