TI Submerged laser micromachining with machine learning for mitigating thermal damage in sustainable polymers
SO PHYSICA SCRIPTA
LA English
DT Article
DE PLA; laser micromachining; machine learning; heat affected zone
ID POLYLACTIC ACID; RAMAN-SPECTRA; FABRICATION; TOOL
AB Nanosecond laser micromachining in polymers often results in an undesirable heat-affected zone (HAZ), impacting machining quality. This study mitigates HAZ by submerging samples in water during laser processing. By optimising the water level, HAZ is effectively eradicated, yielding superior machining precision. Two energy levels are selected to examine the impact of energy and repetition rate on machining quality. Microhardness testing confirmed a significant reduction in HAZ for submerged machining compared to air machining. Laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) analysis revealed higher electron density in the plasma formed in water, suggesting enhanced plasma confinement as a key factor in heat containment and improved machining quality. Raman spectroscopy indicated mild stress-induced changes in air-machined samples, while submerged machining preserved the original bond structure. Additionally, machine learning models, including Random Forest Regressor and Gaussian Process Regressor, are developed to accurately predict Machining Width (MW) and Heat affected Zone (HAZ). A distinct 2D representation of MW and HAZ is generated using Python, providing an effective visualisation or simulation of machining. This study establishes submerged laser micromachining as a viable technique for precision manufacturing, minimising thermal damage while ensuring high-quality microstructures for commercial PLA.
