TI Physics guided machine learning for durability assessment of fiber reinforced polymer bar reinforced seawater and sea-sand concrete beams in marine environments
SO ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Physics guided machine learning; Seawater and sea-sand concrete; Fiber reinforced polymer bar; Beam; Durability assessment
ID RC BEAMS; PERFORMANCE; CORROSION; BEHAVIOR
AB To address the small, noisy, and heterogeneous datasets that hinder durability prediction of fiber reinforced polymer bar reinforced seawater and sea-sand concrete beams in marine environments, this study develops a physics guided machine learning framework for predicting durability related structural responses under marine exposure. The framework combines a physics structured operator for mechanistic guidance, a residual learning module for error correction, and an ensemble based meta knowledge branch for improved feature representation. A curated dataset of 89 beam specimens compiled from seven published studies and one experimental campaign conducted by the authors was used for model development and validation. Compared with conventional tree based models, the proposed framework significantly improves regression accuracy, reducing the root mean square error from 6.16 kN to 2.00 kN for ultimate load and from 4.45 mm to 1.75 mm for ultimate deflection, with corresponding coefficients of determination of 0.998 and 0.977. Additional tests under reduced training size and noise injection further demonstrate strong data efficiency and robustness. Overall, the proposed framework provides an accurate and practical approach for durability assessment of fiber reinforced polymer bar reinforced seawater and sea-sand concrete beams in marine environments.
