TI Modelling and prediction of delamination during the end-milling of glass fibre reinforced polymer composites using response surface methodology and least squares support vector machine
SO PIGMENT & RESIN TECHNOLOGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Modelling; Epoxy resins; Delamination factor; LS SVM
ID PLASTICS; ROUGHNESS; DAMAGE; OPTIMIZATION; PARAMETERS
AB Purpose - The purpose of this study is to develop a mathematical model for predicting the phenomenon of delamination during end milling of glass fibre reinforced polymer (GFRP) composite laminates. Design/methodology/approach - Detailed investigations were carried out based on the response surface methodology (RSM) to evaluate the effect on the delamination and to predict the performance and interactions of the input parameters (cutting speed, feed rate and depth of cut). Least square support vector machine (LS-SVM), a supervised machine learning algorithm, is used to build the predictive model, with the same set of milling parameters considered as model's input and delamination factor as model's output or response. Different tuning settings and regularization of data are used for the analysis and comparison of the model's performance. Findings - In comparison to the regression model developed using RSM, the results of the LS-SVM model show that it is more reliable and accurate in predicting the values of the delamination factor during the end-milling of GFRP composites. Originality/value - Prediction of delamination during the end-milling of GFRP composites using LS-SVM has not been explored previously.
