TI Synergizing machine learning and self-assembly to hypercrosslinked polymers-based carbon nanotubes for supercapacitors
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Self-assembly; Hypercrosslinked polymers; Carbon nanotubes; Supercapacitor
ID CAPACITANCE; ACTIVATION
AB Rapid carbon-neutral industrialization has significantly increased the demand for high-performance supercapacitors. Porous carbon materials have received great attention for their tunable structures and hierarchical porosity, which can be optimized to enhance capacitance. However, further research is needed to elucidate the relationship between pore size, morphology, doping ratio, and performance for practical energy storage applications. To address this challenge, we propose an effective strategy that combines theoretical predictions with insitu self-assembly of hypercrosslinked polymers (HCPs) to design optimal electrode materials. Machine learning identifies key features of carbon materials, while density functional theory calculations confirm the strong K+ adsorption energy (-0.33 eV) from heteroatom incorporation. Guided by these insights, nitrogen, sulfur, and oxygen-doped HCPs with tubular morphology are synthesized via the Friedel-Crafts reaction to meet the identified criteria. Further pyrolysis forms a heteroatom-enriched porous carbon nanotube with a hierarchical structure. It exhibits exceptional performance, achieving a high capacitance of 812.43 F g- 1 at 0.3 A g- 1 in a 6 M KOH solution, along with remarkable cycling stability, retaining 99.84% of its capacitance after 10,000 cycles at 10 A g- 1. This work provides a robust strategy for the rational design of porous carbon for energy storage, leveraging advanced theoretical predictions and assembly techniques.
