TI Advancements in carbon fiber reinforced polymers: Surface modifications for structural and functional excellence
SO SURFACES AND INTERFACES
LA English
DT Article
DE Carbon fibers; Multifunctional composites; Nanocomposites; Polymer-matrix composites
ID INTERFACIAL SHEAR-STRENGTH; ENHANCED MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; EMI SHIELDING EFFECTIVENESS; FIBER/EPOXY COMPOSITES; ELECTROPHORETIC DEPOSITION; FRACTURE-TOUGHNESS; SIZING AGENT; NITRIC-ACID; EPOXY; NANOTUBE
AB Carbon fiber reinforced polymers (CFRPs) are widely used in advanced structural applications due to their exceptional strength-to-weight ratio. However, the inherently inert and non-polar surface of carbon fibers often limits interfacial bonding with polymer matrices, restricting the overall performance of CFRP composites. Surface modification has therefore emerged as a critical strategy to improve interfacial adhesion and enable multifunctional capabilities. This review systematically examines recent advances in carbon fiber surface treatment methods, including coating, grafting, plasma treatment, and oxidation, and evaluates their influence on interfacial bonding and mechanical performance. The mechanisms by which these treatments enhance surface energy, roughness, and chemical compatibility are discussed in detail. In addition to structural reinforcement, the development of multifunctional CFRPs is highlighted, particularly in applications such as electromagnetic interference (EMI) shielding, structural health monitoring (SHM), energy harvesting, and energy storage systems. Recent progress in integrating nanomaterials and advanced surface architectures for enhanced multifunctionality is also summarized. Furthermore, emerging artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning approaches for predicting composite performance and optimizing surface modification processes are discussed. By integrating surface engineering strategies with multifunctional applications, this review provides a comprehensive perspective on the design of next-generation CFRP systems and outlines key challenges and future research directions.
