TI Machine learning-assisted carbon quantum dots-molecularly imprinted polymer array for synchronous screening of multiple sulfonamides
SO SENSORS AND ACTUATORS B-CHEMICAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Sulfonamides; Sensor array; Molecularly imprinted; Multi-target discrimination
ID SENSOR; TETRACYCLINE; ENVIRONMENT; MILK
AB Sulfonamide (SAs) residues in environment and animal-derived food exhibit structural similarity, multi-category and low-concentration characteristics, which pose a substantial challenge to the development of efficient screening methods. Here, we report a fluorescence array comprising four molecularly imprinted polymer-based sensing units for the synchronous determination of 12 common SAs. Each sensing unit employed carbon dots with high quantum yield as the signal source and utilized diverse molecular-sized SA templates to modulate the imprinted cavities and construct differentiated recognition sites. These differentiated recognition cavities and sites enabled the array to produce distinct responses to 12 structurally similar SAs, and generated unique quenching patterns via a charge transfer mechanism. Nine common machine learning algorithms were evaluated and optimized to construct the optimal discriminant model. The Linear Discriminant Analysis showed the optimal classification performance, achieving 98.96 % accuracy in identifying 480 concentration-independent samples, and supporting sensitive quantification of 12 SAs with a detection limit of 0.026 mu g/mL. Furthermore, the array exhibited superior anti-interference ability and practicability, enabling precise screening of multiple real samples, with the results comparable to authoritative methods. This method provides an efficient strategy for the screening of trace SAs and can be extended to the multi-component recognition of other structurally similar pollutants.
