TI Prediction of Properties of Polymer Composite Formulations Using Ensemble Models With Feature Generation
SO JOURNAL OF APPLIED POLYMER SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE composites; dielectric properties; mechanical properties; thermal properties
ID ELECTRICAL-CONDUCTIVITY; CARBON NANOTUBES; STRENGTH; MODULUS; TENSILE; BLACK
AB The development of polymer composites remains largely dependent on trial-and-error experimentation, despite increasingly complex performance requirements in industrial applications. An interpretable machine-learning framework is presented for the simultaneous prediction of nine mechanical, thermal, electrical, and physicochemical properties of polymer composites using an industry-derived dataset comprising 112 commercial products. Unlike most prior studies that focus on a single property or limited material systems, the proposed approach models multiple properties across a broad range of commercially relevant polymers, fillers, and additives. Multiple feature set configurations are systematically evaluated within an ensemble learning framework incorporating Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP)-based feature selection and interpretation, demonstrating that composition-based feature generation capturing nonlinear interactions yields the most robust predictive performance. The resulting models achieve external test set coefficients of determination (R 2) exceeding 0.90 across all properties. SHAP-based interpretation confirms physical consistency with established composite mechanisms, while composition-property contour analyses provide practical guidance for identifying formulation trade-offs among competing properties. The models are implemented in the PolyMIDAS platform, enabling composition-based property prediction and multi-property pre-screening prior to experimental compounding. Overall, this work establishes a practical and interpretable AI-driven framework that supports rational polymer composite design and reduces reliance on costly iterative experimentation.
