TI A comprehensive review of poly(ethylene oxide)-based solid polymer electrolytes: Preparation, ionic transport, failure mechanism and energy storage applications
SO MATERIALS SCIENCE & ENGINEERING R-REPORTS
LA English
DT Review
DE PEO-based electrolytes; Preparation; Ion transport mechanism; Solid-state batteries
ID LITHIUM-METAL BATTERIES; DENDRITIC GROWTH; ELECTROCHEMICAL PERFORMANCE; CONDUCTING MEMBRANE; STATE BATTERIES; PEO ELECTROLYTE; NEURAL-NETWORK; IN-SITU; COPOLYMER; LIQUID
AB Poly(ethylene oxide) (PEO)-based solid polymer electrolytes (SPEs) have garnered considerable attention in the field of all-solid-state batteries (ASSBs). However, challenges such as low room-temperature ionic conductivity and failure behaviors have impeded the practical applications. In this context, this review firstly summarizes the latest developments in the processing-structure-property relationships for PEO-based SPEs. Then, a comprehensive overview and assessment of the mechanisms governing ionic conduction in PEO-based SPEs and different molecular-architectural designation encompassing block copolymerization, cross-linking, blending and composite solid electrolyte (CSEs) are highlighted. Moreover, the failure mechanism of PEO-based SPEs, such as lithium dendrites, decomposition and performance deterioration are discussed further and analyzed by employing cutting-edge characterization techniques. Furthermore, a dedicated comprehension on AI-driven material innovation is supplemented by reviewing the state-of-the-art machine learning (ML) strategies for multi-objective optimizations of PEO-based SPEs. Then, the industrialization challenges (scale-up processing, ultrathin film preparation < 20 mu m, low-temperature performance, cost control) and potential resolutions are opposed for future applications. This review provides a unified theoretical framework and practical guidance for the development of high-performance PEO-based SPEs, which is expected to advance the commercialization of ASSBs with high energy density.
