TI Synergistic construction of supramolecular high-entropy poly(eutectic) conductive elastomers employing hydrogen bonding reinforced factor and coordinable Al(III) cations
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Poly(eutectic) elastomers; Imidazolidinyl urea; Coordination bonds; Sensor; Deep learning; Unconventional luminescence
ID DECOMPOSITION; MEMBRANE; LAYER
AB Constructing a dense, graded, and synergistic crosslinking network is an effective strategy for enhancing the tensile strength and durability of poly(eutectic) elastomers. Given the critical role of hydrogen-bond reinforced factors in high-performance hydrogels, we propose a "one-pot" in-situ polymerization approach for fabricating a series of supramolecular high-entropy poly(eutectic) elastomers, incorporating ultra-dense non-covalent interaction networks formed by imidazolidinyl urea and hierarchical Al-O/Al-N coordination bonds. The resulting elastomers show high transparency (> 82 % optical transmittance), robust tensile strength (up to 4.83 MPa), excellent fatigue resistance (no significant tensile strength reduction after 100 cycles at 10 % strain), and rapid self-healing properties (67 % efficiency within 50 min at 25 degrees C). Moreover, the elastomers demonstrate exceptional antifreeze properties and surface hydrophobicity (water contact angle = 99.38 degrees), attributed to AlO coordination bonds, enhancing durability in humid conditions. As strain sensors, the elastomers exhibit a linear response and high sensitivity over a strain range of 0-100 %, making them suitable for applications in strain sensing, human motion monitoring, micro-expression recognition, Morse code transmission, and handwriting recognition integrated with deep learning. Additionally, the elastomers contain unconventional chromophores with excellent photoluminescence properties, enabling applications in fluorescent anti-counterfeiting, dynamic information storage, and encryption with screen printing technology.
