TI History of chatbots and their emerging applications in textiles, polymers, and sustainability
SO SUSTAINABLE MATERIALS AND TECHNOLOGIES
LA English
DT Article
DE Chatbots; Generative AI; Textiles; Polymer synthesis; Sustainability
ID ARTIFICIAL-INTELLIGENCE; COMMUNICATION; PERFORMANCE; SYSTEM; ELIZA
AB Chatbots have progressed from rule based conversational programs like ELIZA and PARRY to today's complicated AI driven systems that capable of doing multimodal reasoning, domain-specific, and personalized tasks. This review analyses how chatbots have evolved from simple text replies to intelligent, context-aware virtual agents through important milestones in natural language processing, machine learning, and generative AI. Building on this historical foundation, the study investigates the growing significance of chatbots in emerging industrial areas, with a focus on textiles, polymers, and sustainability. Chatbots are being linked to e-commerce platforms, virtual try-on systems, supply chain management, and consumer engagement to provide tailored recommendations, reduce product returns, and improve circularity in the textile and fashion industries. AI-powered conversational agents are enhancing polymer science by facilitating literature reviewing, experimental planning, polymer design, and data-driven decision-making. From a sustainability view, chatbots support life-cycle assessment, carbon tracking, circular economy practices, and communication across supply chains. Market analysis shows strong growth through 2030, with the global conversational AI market expected to exceed USD 41 billion. At the same time, challenges remain including the need for domain-specific models, better explainability, reliable outputs, data integration, and ethical use. This review also sets directions for future research, such as building specialized chatbot systems, linking them with materials databases, defining clear performance benchmarks, and encouraging collaboration across disciplines.
