PT J
AU Kumar, G
   Punetha, D
   Pandey, SK
AF Kumar, Gulshan
   Punetha, Deepak
   Pandey, Saurabh Kumar
TI Performance analysis of polymer electrolyte membrane fuel cells: Insights from sensor-based monitoring and machine learning
SO JOURNAL OF POWER SOURCES
LA English
DT Article
DE Electrolyte potential; PEMFC; Nitrogen concentration; Humidity effect; COMSOL; Machine learning
AB This study investigates the electrochemical performance of a polymer electrolyte membrane fuel cell (PEMFC) under varying conditions of humidity, nitrogen concentration, and cell voltage, utilizing experimental data, COMSOL simulations, and machine learning modeling to characterize the device response. Results indicate pure oxygen enhances fuel cell performance, with oxygen mole fraction increasing from 0.25 to 0.30 at a mole fraction of Nitrogen (xN2 ) = 0, while dropping to 0.054-0.063 at xN2 = 0.79, a reduction of approximately 80 %. Polynomial regression achieves Coefficient of Determination (R2) scores of 0.9989 for air and oxygen, while humidified oxygen enhances current densities. Humidified oxygen as the oxidant enhances current density by 25 %, reaching 1.25 A/cm2 at 0.75 V, whereas humidified air results in a lower current density of 0.95 A/cm2. Realtime monitoring using embedded micro-sensors reveals critical insights into temperature, flow rate, and pressure distributions within PEMFCs, enabling better understanding and optimization of internal processes. Electrolyte potential dropped from 0.35 V to 0.21 V at xN2 = 0.79, and a polynomial regression (degree 3) model shows high accuracy with R2 scores of 0.9989 for air and oxygen. This work highlights the impact of reactant composition and operating voltage on fuel cell efficiency, providing insights for optimizing fuel cell design and operation.
