TI Flame-retardant conductive elastomers for ultra-durable wearable strain sensing
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Flexible strain sensing; Flame-retardant elastomer; Free volume; Self-extinguishing
ID GLASS-TRANSITION TEMPERATURE; SENSORS; MODEL
AB Flexible sensing technologies are pivotal for real-time physiological monitoring and predictive safety interventions in mission-critical scenarios such as firefighting and industrial hazard mitigation. However, conventional sensors often fail catastrophically above 200 degrees C or in corrosive gases (e.g., SO2, H2S) due to thermal degradation of their conductive pathways. We propose that this failure originates from high-temperatureinduced free-volume expansion in polymeric matrices, which undermines both structural and functional integrity. To address this, we constructed a free-volume-constrained conductive network within a flame-retardant elastomer, yielding a self-extinguishing wearable strain sensor. The key innovation lies in covalently crosslinking MXene nanosheets with amino-functionalized carbon nanotubes to suppress thermally driven freevolume expansion. The resulting sensor delivers stable strain signal transmission at 200 degrees C and in corrosive gaseous environments, with signal resolution and retention surpassing those of state-of-the-art devices. Mechanically, it accommodates a wide operational range under high-temperature conditions-from 150% macroscopic deformation down to 0.1% micro-vibrations-ensuring high-fidelity acquisition of physiological signals. By integrating strain-signal feature extraction with machine learning algorithms, we further develop personalized biometric models capable of predicting firefighter fatigue states in real time. This work establishes a reliable platform for physiological monitoring in extreme environments, paving the way for enhanced real-time safety interventions and operational risk mitigation.
