TI Phosphorus-based polymeric scale inhibitors for energy and water industries: Synthesis, inhibition mechanism, and field deployment
SO DESALINATION
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymeric scale inhibitor; Oil and gas production; Desalination; Squeeze treatment; Mechanism; Field deployment
ID PHOSPHINO-POLYCARBOXYLIC ACID; BARIUM-SULFATE SCALE; POLYASPARTIC ACID; PERMEATE RECOVERY; MINERAL SCALE; SQUEEZE; OIL; CACO3; CARBONATE; ADSORPTION
AB The formation of inorganic scale poses significant operational and economic challenges in both oil and gas production and desalination systems, leading to reduced efficiency, increased maintenance costs, and potential system failure. Phosphorus-based polymeric scale inhibitors have emerged as a promising class of materials that combine the strong metal-binding affinity of phosphorus functional groups with the structural versatility and multi-functionality of polymeric systems. This review provides a comprehensive and integrated overview of these advanced inhibitors, with a focus on their synthesis, inhibition mechanisms, and industrial deployment across energy and water industries. Recent advances in polymer synthesis have enabled the design of a wide range of phosphorus-containing polymers, including homo-polymers, copolymers, dendrimers, and functionalized materials, with tunable molecular architectures and improved performance. The mechanisms of scale inhibition are discussed in detail, highlighting the synergistic roles of various effects. Particular attention is given to the application of these inhibitors in oilfield squeeze treatments and continuous injection strategies, as well as their use as antiscalants in reverse osmosis and thermal desalination processes. Despite their superior performance, challenges remain in terms of environmental impact, structure-performance relationships, and field applicability. Promising directions to address these challenges include the development of low-phosphorus polymers and the integration of machine learning for molecular optimization. This review provides insights to guide the development of next-generation, efficient, and sustainable scale control technologies.
