TI Sustainable bamboo-nettle hybrid polymer composites: advances in fiber modification, fabrication, and property optimization
SO COMPOSITE INTERFACES
LA English
DT Review; Early Access
DE Bamboo fiber; nettle fiber; natural fiber composite; interfacial adhesion; hybridization; hand lay-up; sustainable materials
AB Natural-fiber-reinforced polymer composites are gaining traction as sustainable alternatives to glass- and carbon-fiber systems, yet bamboo and nettle two agriculturally advantageous, mechanically promising fibers, remain comparatively underexplored as a hybrid reinforcement pair. This review consolidates recent advances in bamboo-nettle hybrid polymer composites, synthesizing evidence on raw fiber properties, polymer matrix compatibility, interfacial surface modification, fabrication technologies, hybridization architectures, and resulting structure-property relationships. Hybridization is reported to yield tensile, flexural, and impact improvements in the general range of 20-40% over single-fiber laminates through synergistic stress transfer, although this range is drawn predominantly from bamboo-glass, flax-glass, and other analogous natural-natural and natural-synthetic hybrid systems rather than from bamboo-nettle laminates specifically, while alkali and other chemical/physical surface treatments improve fiber-matrix adhesion and reduce moisture-related degradation. Vacuum-assisted fabrication routes outperform conventional hand lay-up by minimizing voids and improving resin distribution, and comparative benchmarking of bamboo and nettle against established bast fibers suggests these systems could approach the mechanical performance of glass-fiber-reinforced polymer at lower density. Despite this progress, challenges persist in property variability, standardization, long-term environmental durability, and scalable manufacturing. Future work should prioritize bio-based matrix development, advanced surface engineering, automated fabrication, and computational/machine-learning-assisted design to enable structure-property optimization.
