TI High-entropy heterogeneous design enables ultra-fatigue-resistant gel elastomers for multimodal sensing and intelligent human-machine interaction
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Gel elastomers; Low-hysteresis; Sensing; Electronic skin; Machine learning
AB In frontier fields such as flexible electronics, wearable devices, and soft robotics, sustaining low hysteresis and high toughness in gel elastomers during continuous loading-unloading cycles is crucial for achieving precise dynamic interactions and long-term stable operation. However, simultaneously meeting this dual requirement remains challenging due to the intrinsic trade-off between energy dissipation and elastic energy storage. This work proposes a high-entropy heterogeneous design, the amphiphilic structure of NIPAm effectively increases the entropy change associated with intermolecular interactions by introducing hydrophobic association and multiple competitive binding pathways. In parallel, hydroxypropyl cellulose (HPC) forms dense chain entanglements that strengthen topological constraints within the network. Together with dynamic crosslinking interactions, including metal coordination, hierarchical hydrogen bonding, and reversible hydrophobic associations, these features construct a multiscale heterogeneous network with tunable energy dissipation pathways. The regulation of entropic and enthalpic interactions endows the gel elastomers with high toughness (3.43 MPa, 16.46 MJ m-3) and ultra-low hysteresis (2.6%). The material withstands over 120,000 cycles at 200% strain without significant degradation, exhibiting state-of-the-art fatigue life. When integrated into humanmachine collaborative interaction systems and combined with deep learning technology, it enables highprecision motion recognition (99.43% accuracy) and interactive feedback, providing a novel material solution for emerging fields like human-machine interaction.
