TI Electrochemical sensor-integrated omics-driven data framework for performance optimization of polymer-based alkali-activated concrete
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Alkali-activated concrete; Polymer-modified concrete; Electrochemical sensors; Materials omics; Machine learning; Smart infrastructure
AB The increasing environmental concerns associated with conventional Portland cement production have prompted extensive research into developing alternative sustainable construction material options, such as polymer-based alkali-activated concrete (PAAC). Due to complex interactions among a number of factors including type of polymer modifiers and amount used, nature and content of the alkali activator and precursor materials, curing conditions during early age hardening stage and long-term durability parameters in later service life stages, it remains challenging to optimize performance. In this study, an electrochemical sensor-integrated omics-driven data framework is proposed that combines embedded electrochemical sensing technologies (such as corrosion potential monitoring technology, ionic transport behavior analysis through electrical conductivity measurements during curing, moisture dynamics detection by impedance and capacitance sensors in the early age of hydration stage and microstructural evolution tracking using nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for service life) to characterize large-scale physicochemical interactions governing material performance with omics-driven data integration (including materials omics, process omics, durability omics), as well as predictive data analytics using advanced machine learning algorithms that can identify key indicators of interest in predicting the effects on composition variables and environmental conditions on structural properties. Experimental investigations have revealed significant improvements to prediction accuracy for compressive strength, chloride resistance, permeability reduction, long-term durability by combining embedded electrochemical sensing technologies with an omics-driven data framework; this establishes a digital twin platform that may lead to next-generation smart infrastructure systems.
