TI Polymer electrolytes for solid-state lithium batteries: A review of ionic conductivity, mechanical robustness, and interfacial stability towards practical applications
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING SCIENCE
LA English
DT Review
DE Solid polymer electrolytes; Solid-state lithium batteries; Ionic conductivity; Interfacial stability; Mechanical robustness
ID POLY(ETHYLENE OXIDE); METAL ANODE; TRANSPORT; CHALLENGES; DYNAMICS
AB Solid polymer electrolytes (SPEs) are promising candidates for intrinsically safe high-energy solid-state lithium batteries (SSLBs), but the progress has been hindered by three intertwined challenges: low ionic conductivity at ambient temperature (<10(-4 )S cm(-1)), fundamental compromise between mechanical robustness and chain mobility, and poor interfacial stability against lithium anodes and high-voltage cathodes. We comprehensively review advances that move beyond individual property optimization to integrated strategies that address all three pillars simultaneously. We systematically analyze polymer engineering (dynamic crosslinking, copolymerization), composite electrolytes with functional fillers, polymer-in-salt systems, and interface engineering by artificial SEI layers and in-situ polymerization. The key insight is that these properties are intrinsically coupled: improved conductivity usually comes at the expense of mechanical integrity, and rigidification hinders ion transport. There are some integrated designs that have true synergy. For example, dynamic metal-ligand networks boost the modulus by 133-fold with little loss of conductivity, core-shell nanofiber architectures form continuous Li+ highways and simultaneously fortify the mechanics, and dual-additive systems achieve stable 4.5 V pouch cells with > 95% capacity retention. Despite these advances, critical gaps still exist in ambient temperature operation and high current density (>3 mA cm(- 2)) in practical cells. We conclude that hierarchical multifunctional design, dynamic polymer networks, and machine learning-accelerated discovery are the key to reconciling conductivity, mechanical integrity, and interfacial chemistry for commercially viable SPE-based SSLBs.
