PT J
AU Kaiser, R
   Polycarpou, AA
AF Kaiser, Rashed
   Polycarpou, Andreas A.
TI From molecular architecture to system performance: polymer compatibility in hydrogen energy systems
SO EUROPEAN POLYMER JOURNAL
LA English
DT Review
DE Hydrogen-compatible polymers; Molecular architecture framework; Hydrogen permeation and diffusion; Polymer degradation mechanisms; Hybrid polymer composites; High-pressure hydrogen storage
ID GAS PERMEATION; FREE-VOLUME; SEGMENTAL DYNAMICS; STRAIN-RATE; PERMEABILITY; TRANSITION; DIFFUSIVITY; MEMBRANES; EXPOSURE; BEHAVIOR
AB Hydrogen is a promising clean energy carrier; however, ensuring the long-term reliability of polymeric materials in hydrogen environments remains a major challenge because hydrogen transport, mechanical degradation, and microstructural evolution are strongly coupled. Existing reviews primarily classify polymers according to chemistry or individual properties but lack a unified framework linking molecular architecture to hydrogen compatibility. This review proposes a qualitative four-pillar molecular architecture framework based on hydrogen bonding, free-volume distribution, crystallinity/morphology, and segmental mobility to systematically interpret polymer behavior under high-pressure molecular hydrogen environments relevant to storage, transportation, and sealing applications. The framework distinguishes physical degradation mechanisms, including hydrogen sorption, plasticization, swelling, and rapid gas decompression, from chemical degradation processes occurring in reactive hydrogen environments. Representative polymer classes are critically compared to elucidate the relationships among molecular architecture, hydrogen transport, mechanical integrity, and long-term durability. Recent advances in polymer blends, nanofillers, multilayer liners, and hybrid composites are also reviewed, with clear distinction between hydrogen-specific experimental evidence and promising design strategies requiring further validation. In addition, key failure mechanisms unique to composite systems, including interfacial debonding, matrix cracking, delamination, and rapid gas decompression damage, are critically discussed. Finally, future research directions are proposed, emphasizing standardized hydrogen testing, multiscale characterization, molecular simulations, and machine-learning-assisted materials design. The proposed framework provides a mechanistic foundation for the rational selection and development of next-generation hydrogencompatible polymers while serving as a conceptual guide for future quantitative structure-property relationships.
