TI A review on mechanical properties and prediction models of fiber-reinforced polymer composites at elevated temperatures
SO JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Review
DE FRP composites; Thermal degradation; Strength degradation; Constitutive modeling; Machine learning
ID VARYING STRAIN RATES; FRP BARS; TENSILE PERFORMANCE; STRENGTH DEGRADATION; BASALT FIBER; BEHAVIOR; GFRP; GLASS; CFRP; CONCRETE
AB Fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP) composites are widely used in civil infrastructure, yet their mechanical reliability under fire and high-temperature conditions remains a critical limitation. This review synthesizes current knowledge on the thermo-mechanical behavior, degradation mechanisms, and predictive models of GFRP, CFRP, and BFRP exposed to elevated temperatures. Experimental data from bars and laminates indicate glass transition temperatures of 80-120 degrees C and critical temperatures of approximately 300-330 degrees C for bars and 200-300 degrees C for laminates, at which tensile strength typically reduces to about 50 % of ambient values. Above 450 degrees C, strength retention generally falls below 20 %, whereas elastic modulus exhibits lower sensitivity, highlighting distinct stiffness-strength degradation patterns. Failure mechanisms are governed by fiber type, resin chemistry, fire-protective coatings, and specimen geometry, encompassing matrix softening, fiber-matrix debonding, resin combustion, and fiber oxidation. BFRP and phenolicbased systems consistently demonstrate superior thermal stability compared with GFRP and CFRP. The review further evaluates empirical and semi-empirical constitutive models, including sigmoid, hyperbolic tangent, kinetic, and mixture-rule formulations, and contrasts them with machine-learning approaches such as ANN, RF, XGBoost, and hybrid neuro-fuzzy models. Although data-driven methods improve predictive accuracy, their applicability is constrained by limited, fragmented, and non-standardized datasets. Future research may prioritize thermally robust resin systems, engineered interfaces, multiscale constitutive modeling, and AI-driven, datacentric design supported by comprehensive databases to enable reliable performance-based fire design of FRP-strengthened and FRP-based structures.
