TI Continuous blood pressure monitoring based on improved multi-scale U-net assisted with piezoelectric polymer nanocomposite sensors
SO BIOMEDICAL SIGNAL PROCESSING AND CONTROL
LA English
DT Article
DE Blood pressure; Photoplethysmography; PVDF; MXene; Deep learning; MLSU_net network
AB Objective: Continuous, real-time monitoring of arterial pressure is essential for effective management of hypertension and related diseases. However, existing techniques are limited by size and accuracy, restricting earlystage continuous blood pressure monitoring. Our work aims to present flexible sensors for long-term pulse wave monitoring and propose a deep learning model predicting blood pressure waveforms from a single-channel photoplethysmographic (PPG) signals. Methods: This study develops a flexible piezoelectric sensor by integrating poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) with MXene filler (PM) for improved sensing. Subsequently, MLSU-net based on U-net is proposed to estimate blood pressure, using multi-scale convolutional kernels within the upsampling-downsampling modules and integrates a long short-term memory (LSTM) network with a selective kernel (SK) attention at skip-connections. Results: The flexible sensor provides reliable monitoring with minimal impact from human movements, demonstrating rapid response (50.7 ms). The MLSU-net model achieves accurate blood pressure predictions with mean absolute errors (MAE) f standard deviation (SD) of 2.95 f 4.19, 2.13 f 3.08, and 5.03 f 6.16 mmHg for systolic blood pressure (SBP), mean blood pressure (MBP), and diastolic blood pressure (DBP). This approach meets the British Hypertension Society (BHS) Grade A standard and the Association for the Advancement of Medical Instrumentation (AAMI) standard. Conclusions: The proposed method avoids data screening with superior performance and enables precise, continuous blood pressure monitoring in real-world settings, providing a promising solution for hypertension management outside clinical environments.
