TI Machine learning supported evolutionary optimization for multi-objective reverse engineering of radical polymerizations
SO COMPUTERS & CHEMICAL ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Machine learning; Kinetic monte carlo simulation; Reverse engineering; Multi-objective optimization; Genetic algorithms
ID GENETIC ALGORITHM; BUTYL ACRYLATE; BETA-SCISSION; DESIGN
AB Creating polymers with desired properties is a very challenging task solved by reverse engineering of polymerization processes considering monomers with a complex reaction mechanism, for example such as acrylates. The process involves a large number of contradicting objectives, constraints and limitations that are intractable using conventional simulation or machine learning (ML) techniques. In this study, it is formulated as a multi-objective optimization (MOO) problem, which is assisted by ML models to predict the properties of interest. ML models are trained on data obtained from kinetic Monte Carlo simulations to minimize the number of expensive laboratory experiments. A genetic algorithm (GA) is used as the MOO optimizer, considering reaction time, monomer conversion and molar mass distribution similarity as objectives. The trained ML models predict polymer properties for recipes generated by the GA, allowing for rapid evaluation of the fitness function and the generation of novel, previously non-simulated polymerization recipes. Furthermore, the most suitable recipes are selected from the resulting Pareto front based on user priorities, considering user-defined weights for each objective. The approach is validated for two scenarios of butyl acrylate radical polymerizations with different types of initiation (chemically and self-initiated), number of parameters, and shapes of molar mass distributions. The results show that the ML-based GA-MOO approach efficiently identifies optimal recipes with high accuracy for different focuses. Even training of the ML models with a limited amount of data, is associated with excellent performance. The proposed fast and cost-effective solution for reverse engineering is suggested to be applicable for other polymerization systems.
