TI A Data-Driven Approach to the Estimation of Charge Transport Parameters in Polymeric Dielectrics
SO IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON DIELECTRICS AND ELECTRICAL INSULATION
LA English
DT Article
DE Mathematical models; Space charge; Computational modeling; Data models; Noise; Dielectrics; Spatial resolution; Heuristic algorithms; Charge measurement; Noise measurement; Bipolar charge transport (BCT); dielectrics; LDPE; physics-informed machine learning (PIML); pulsed-electroacoustic (PEA) measurement; space charge
ID SPACE-CHARGE; MODEL
AB Analytical and numerical techniques are used to study the charge dynamics in insulation systems. These methods are complex and computationally tedious. In recent years, physics-informed machine learning (PIML) methods such as the sparse identification of nonlinear dynamics (SINDy) algorithm have been used to formulate appropriate partial differential equations (PDEs) to describe dynamical systems. Such methods are able to rapidly estimate parameters with a reduction in computational complexity. In this work, a methodology for the application of the SINDy algorithm to the charge transport in polymeric dielectrics is developed. The equations of charge transport (such as the nonlinear convection-diffusion equations) and the charge transport parameters (such as mobility and diffusivity) are discovered (rather than assumed) from the space charge data, using the SINDy algorithm. The methodology is first developed using synthesized computational data. It is shown that the spatial/temporal resolution of input data impacts the discovery of nonlinear-PDEs and consequently the estimation of sparse coefficients using SINDy. At high spatiotemporal resolutions of input data, the SINDy model proves to be over 90% accurate; however, accuracy decreases with input data resolution. Finally, the model is applied to space charge data experimentally obtained from pulsed-electroacoustic (PEA) measurements. Despite the low resolution of PEA data, the SINDy algorithm correctly identifies important features of the charge transport phenomenon from experimental data. The estimates are validated against computational data obtained from a bipolar charge transport (BCT) model. This study illustrates the potential of data-driven techniques to study the space charge phenomena in dielectrics.
