TI Experimental investigation of tribological and dielectric behavior in hybrid polymer nanocomposites for insulated bearings with machine learning assisted optimization
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF POLYMER ANALYSIS AND CHARACTERIZATION
LA English
DT Article; Early Access
DE Nanocomposites; XRD; SEM; tribology; machine learning; dielectric property
ID MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; GRAPHENE OXIDE; POLYETHERETHERKETONE; COMPOSITE
AB The proposed study aims at creating hybrid nanocomposites of Graphene Oxide (GO) and Copper (Cu) nanoparticles reinforced in Polyether ether ketone (PEEK) to apply in multifunctional uses in high-speed electromechanical systems (mostly insulated bearings). It is targeted to improve tribological and dielectric characteristics of PEEK, which has a high insulation and wear capability. Hybrid nanocomposites containing 5 wt.% GO and 1-5 wt.% Cu were prepared and analyzed in terms of the dielectric constant (epsilon ') and dielectric loss (epsilon ''), wear rate, and coefficient of friction. SEM, EDS, XRD, and FTIR were used to measure structural and morphological characteristics. The interaction of GO and Cu contributed 88% for wear and 93% for COF and 10% for dielectric response due to synergetic interaction. Extra Trees, XGBoost, and Random Forest machine learning models were used to predict epsilon [prime] and epsilon [inverted-c], and Extra Trees had the best accuracy of 99.99% and generalization on the various data splits. The findings have shown that 2 wt.% of Cu composites produced the highest epsilon' (similar to 4.3) since interfacial polarization was increased, and 3 & 4 wt.% of Cu composites provided the best stable epsilon ''. In general, bearing insulation materials must withstand simultaneous mechanical wear and electrical stress during operational conditions in relevant applications. The GO-Cu/PEEK hybrid nanocomposites improve wear resistance by reducing friction-induced surface damage while maintaining stable dielectric behavior through controlled filler incorporation. Together, these improvements support the potential of GO-Cu/PEEK nanocomposites as next-generation bearing insulation system materials.
