TI Data-driven computational and machine learning design of molecularly imprinted polymer sensors for phenolic contaminants
SO TRAC-TRENDS IN ANALYTICAL CHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Review
DE Molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs); Computational chemistry; Density functional theory (DFT); Artificial intelligence (AI); Machine learning (ML); Chemical sensors; Phenolic contaminants
ID SOLID-PHASE EXTRACTION; PERFORMANCE; MIP
AB Phenolic contaminants constitute an environmentally and biologically relevant class of pollutants due to their persistence, toxicity, and widespread occurrence in industrial effluents, food systems, and aquatic ecosystems. Reliable monitoring remains analytically challenging because of structural similarity and trace-level concentrations in complex matrices. Although various analytical approaches have been developed, achieving high selectivity in complex environmental and food samples remains difficult. In this context, molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) sensors have emerged as promising recognition platforms, offering selective, robust, and chemically stable alternatives to biological receptors for phenolic detection. Despite these advantages, traditional MIP development still relies heavily on empirical optimization, limiting design predictability, binding-site homogeneity, and reproducibility. To facilitate the transition toward rational MIP design, computational chemistry and artificial intelligence (AI) enable molecular-level interrogation of template-monomer interactions, prepolymerization assemblies, and binding-site architecture before experimental synthesis. Accordingly, this review critically examines recent advances in computationally guided MIP sensor development for phenolic contaminants, highlighting molecular docking, density functional theory (DFT), and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations. Emphasis is placed on multiscale modeling integrating electronic structure, solvent effects, and cavity dynamics to support rational imprinting. Complementing these approaches, machine learning (ML) and AI establish nonlinear structure-performance relationships from molecular descriptors, formulation variables, and experimental data, enabling monomer prioritization, formulation optimization, binding-affinity prediction, and predictive MIP design. Current challenges, including force-field accuracy, dataset scarcity, model transferability, and reproducibility, are critically discussed. Finally, future perspectives emphasize integrated digital design pipelines, hybrid physics-ML workflows, autonomous optimization, and intelligent sensing platforms for realtime phenolic monitoring.
