TI Deflection prediction of lightweight foamed concrete T-beams reinforced with glass fiber reinforced polymer bars: Proposed model using machine learning
SO STRUCTURES
LA English
DT Article
DE Lightweight foamed concrete; Glass FRP; Deflection; Numerical analysis; Machine learning; Parametric study; Proposed model
ID FLEXURAL BEHAVIOR; GFRP; STEEL; SERVICEABILITY; PERFORMANCE; STRENGTH
AB Design guidelines show noticeable differences in predicting the deflection of lightweight foamed concrete (LWFC) T-beams reinforced with glass fiber-reinforced polymer (GFRP) bars. To address these inconsistencies, a new model has been proposed to improve deflection predictions for LWFC T-beams. To support the development of this model, an experimental investigation was conducted involving four LWFC T-beams and one normalweight concrete (NWC) T-beam. This investigation examined the effects of reinforcement ratio, reinforcement type, and concrete type. Finite element analysis (FEA) was performed using ANSYS software. The numerical results closely matched the experimental trends. Machine learning (ML) techniques were applied to identify the parameters affecting deflection. Several supervised learning models were trained and evaluated using a dataset that combined experimental results with data extracted from the literature. Feature importance rankings from these models guided the selection of key parameters for a parametric study. Subsequently, a parametric study was conducted using FEA to assess the effects of shear span-to-depth ratio, modulus of elasticity of GFRP bars, GFRP reinforcement ratio, concrete compressive strength, and flange-to-web width ratio. Finally, a proposed model to predict deflection was developed and validated against five code provisions (ACI 440.1R-06, ACI 440.1R-15, CSA S806-12, ISIS, and ECP 208-05), as well as three empirical models from the literature. The deflections predicted by the proposed model showed strong agreement with the actual deflections, yielding mean ratios of 0.98 and 1.00, with coefficients of variation of 0.20 and 0.14 at the service and ultimate load levels, respectively.
