TI A machine learning-DFT assisted structural screening for efficient polymer donors for their potential photovoltaic material applications
SO MATERIALS CHEMISTRY AND PHYSICS
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer donors; Bandgap; Random forest; Organic photovoltaics; Indoline
ID WATER-SPLITTING PHOTOCATALYST; SOLAR
AB In this study, a machine learning (ML) assisted prediction of small donor molecules to screen for their low bandgaps in photovoltaic (PV) polymers has been made. For this, a dataset of 563 molecules from literature with their bandgaps is collected. Their molecular descriptors are calculated with RDKit tool of Python Program. The descriptors show a strong negative correlation for its zero-order molecular connectivity (chi vo) and aromatic rings which indicates a delicate balance of its electronic properties. Among various tested ML models, The Random Forest model emerges as the top-performing model after achieving an R-squared (R2) value of 0.86 for explaining 86 % of the variance in the data. Its Topological Polar Surface Area (TPSA) and MolLogP values demonstrate notable importance with feature importance of 0.09 and 0.07. Its bandgap values distribute across its t-SNE map in a non-linear fashion having higher values (>4 eV) to associate with fewer aromatic rings and lower molecular weights, while regions with lower bandgap values (<2 eV) are characterized with complex ring systems and higher molecular weights. From the analysis, indoline-based donors emerge with the lowest bandgap values. Its conjugated regions comprise carbon-carbon it bonds and adjacent it* orbitals with nitrogen-containing regions to participate in LP-it* transitions. All These findings provide valuable insights for designing new polymers electronic properties. It also makes indoline donors an ideal candidate for organic PVs. This research can open ways for developing high-performance materials, paving the way for innovative applications in organic electronics.
