TI Defect-connectivity-governed failure mechanisms and surrogate-assisted optimization of FDM-printed polymer lattices
SO ENGINEERING FAILURE ANALYSIS
LA English
DT Article
DE Architected structures; Additive manufacturing; Stress field; Manufacturing-induced defects; Machine learning
AB Additively manufactured polymer lattices are often limited by mechanical anisotropy, manufacturing-induced defects, and unpredictable failure. Although previous studies have mainly focused on infill density, material composition, or printing-parameter optimization, the role of defect topology in governing failure behavior remains insufficiently understood. Here, a defectdriven failure-analysis framework is established to reveal how infill architecture, defect connectivity, and load-transfer pathways determine the mechanical reliability of FDM-printed polymer lattices. Multi-axial mechanical tests on PLA, ABS, PETG, and PETG-CF are combined with SEM fracture characterization and finite element simulations. The results show that structural reliability is governed not simply by defect density or relative density, but by the geometric connectivity of defect networks and topology-controlled stress transfer. Surface-continuous TPMS architectures generate diffuse stress fields and reduce defect correlation, promoting progressive collapse and stable energy absorption. In contrast, stretch-dominated FCC lattices provide efficient axial load transfer but are susceptible to nodal stress concentration and local instability, revealing a design paradox in which efficient load paths may also become efficient failure paths. Furthermore, a machine-learning-assisted optimization strategy is developed for the FCC lattice using unit-cell size and strut thickness as design variables. Gaussian process regression is coupled with NSGA-II to obtain Pareto-optimal designs. The optimized FCC configuration increases SEA and MCF by 14.0% and 15.1%, respectively, while maintaining nearly unchanged relative density. This study demonstrates that defect connectivity is a governing factor in FDM-printed lattice failure and that geometric optimization offers an effective route for improving energy-absorption reliability.
