TI Deep learning-assisted intelligent fluorescence sensor incorporating perovskite quantum dots with dual-recognition imprinting polymers for ultrasensitive and high-selectivity kanamycin detection
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Perovskite quantum dots; Dual-recognition molecularly imprinted; polymers; Kanamycin; Ratiometric fluorescence and deep learning; assisted intelligent detection
AB Kanamycin (KANA) overuse threatens public health, necessitating precise and portable detection approaches. To address this challenge, a ratiometric fluorescence sensor (TB-Eu-MOFs@MIPs@CsPbBr3-Rb) was innovatively developed for ultrasensitive and selective detection of KANA. This sensor integrated oriented molecular imprinting on TB-Eu-MOFs with in-situ encapsulation of CsPbBr3-Rb perovskite quantum dots. High-affinity imprinting layer featuring covalent/non-covalent dual-recognition sites boosted the precise identification of TB-Eu-MOFs@MIPs@CsPbBr3-Rb to KANA. Thanks to synergistic dual-mechanism of dynamic quenching and electron transfer, water-resistant CsPbBr3-Rb (520 nm) underwent pronounced fluorescence quenching upon exposure to KANA, boosting sensitivity. Conversely, the red fluorescence (620 nm) of TB-Eu-MOFs remained unaffected. Given inherent merits, TB-Eu-MOFs@MIPs@CsPbBr3-Rb demonstrated exceptional suitability for ratiometric fluorescence profiling of KANA, featuring a linear range of 0.075-24.000 mu g/mL, a detection limit of 22.5 ng/mL, a superior imprinting factor (6.66), ultra-fast response (2 min), attractive green-yellow-pale pinkred transformation, and satisfactory recoveries (90.00-98.43%). Intriguingly, by integrating a deep learningdriven smartphone device, the proposed sensor achieved on-site identification, concentration classification, and quantitative assay of KANA, demonstrating commendable precision and desirable recoveries (90.47-109.85%) in tap water, milk, and chicken. Accordingly, the deep learning-driven fluorescence sensor exhibited portability, feasibility, high selectivity, and sensitivity, offering a powerful tool for on-site food safety and environmental monitoring with high practicality.
