TI Charge transfer-enhanced OECT glucose sensor with superabsorbent polymer composites for non-invasive breath analysis
SO SENSORS AND ACTUATORS B-CHEMICAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Glucose sensors; Charge transfer-enhanced; Organic electrochemical transistors; Exhaled breath analysis; Wearable health monitoring
AB To address the limitations of traditional blood glucose monitoring in industrial environments, this study presents a charge transfer-enhanced glucose sensor based on organic electrochemical transistor, utilizing a carboxymethyl cellulose-modified PEDOT:PSS with aminated multi-walled carbon nanotubes/gold nanoparticles composite film. The sensor is integrated with superabsorbent polymer composites for non-invasive breath analysis. Molecular electrostatic potential calculations reveal that hydrogen-bond networks between carboxymethyl cellulose carboxyl groups and glucose hydroxyl moieties, together with it-it interactions of glucose molecules at the composite surface, enhance interfacial charge transfer efficiency by 2.5 % (0.236e -> 0.242e). Structural characterization confirms a honeycomb-like porous architecture (0.5-2 mu m pore size) formed via carboxymethyl cellulose-mediated hydrogen bonding, accelerating glucose diffusion while suppressing interferent adsorption (response<1 %). The composite's three-dimensional conductive network combines PEDOT:PSS (p-type pathways), carbon nanotubes (surface area expansion) and gold nanoparticles (catalytic sites), achieving a linear detection range of 2-22 <mu>mol center dot L-1 (extendable up to 9 mM) in exhaled breath condensate with 56.656 mu A center dot (mmol center dot L-1 center dot cm(2))-1 sensitivity, 0.2 mu mol center dot L-1 detection limit (S/N = 23.8), and < 4.8 s response time. The sensorintegrated facemask platform uses superabsorbent polymer to maintain 100 % relative humidity, stabilizing the electrolyte layer for real-time monitoring. Machine learning (random forest classifier processing 18 timefrequency features from transistor outputs) achieves 87.5 % accuracy in resolving glucose fluctuations. This work demonstrates the integration of hierarchical materials, interfacial charge control, and algorithmic processing for wearable biosensors in health monitoring.
